# ==========================================
# CI GARBAGE COLLECTION
# ==========================================
# The size cap below is only a fallback for bazel run by hand on a CI host.
# Every CI step sources .buildkite/steps/ci-env.sh, which computes a cap from
# the filesystem that actually holds the disk cache (80% of its total size)
# and passes it on the command line after --config=ci-<os>, where it overrides
# this value. 3T only ever fitted the 3.6 TB office hosts: on a smaller disk
# the size-based GC never fires and the cache fills the disk, and an agent on
# a full disk stays connected and accepts jobs it never runs. 3T is kept rather
# than lowered because a hand-run command that evicts nothing is harmless,
# whereas a small fallback would evict most of a 3.6 TB host's cache. See
# .buildkite/linux-agents-runbook.md, "Disk-cache GC sizing".
build:ci --experimental_disk_cache_gc_max_size=3T
build:ci --experimental_disk_cache_gc_max_age=60d
build:ci --experimental_disk_cache_gc_idle_delay=5m

# ==========================================
# CI ENVIRONMENT OPTIMIZATIONS
# ==========================================
build:ci --color=yes
build:ci --curses=no
build:ci --show_progress_rate_limit=5.0
build:ci --announce_rc

# max_idle_secs is a bazel STARTUP option, so a `startup:ci` line here would
# only take effect if something passed `--config=ci` as a startup flag (i.e.
# before the subcommand) -- nothing does; `--config=ci-<os>` is always passed
# as a build/test flag after the subcommand, which only reaches `build:ci`/
# `test:ci` lines. The idle TTL (--max_idle_secs=1800, the same for every CI
# pipeline and OS) is set on the command line instead -- see
# CI_BAZEL_STARTUP_FLAGS in .buildkite/steps/ci-env.sh. That command-line
# flag takes precedence over the local default of 7200s (2h) set by
# `startup --max_idle_secs=7200` in the root .bazelrc.

# Linux cache configuration
build:ci-linux --config=ci
build:ci-linux --repository_cache=/mnt/ssd/bazel/repo_cache
build:ci-linux --disk_cache=/mnt/ssd/bazel/disk_cache


# Darwin cache configuration
build:ci-darwin --config=ci
build:ci-darwin --repository_cache=/Volumes/ssd/bazel/repo_cache
build:ci-darwin --disk_cache=/Volumes/ssd/bazel/disk_cache

# Pin the Xcode toolchain on the darwin CI fleet. Two incidents in one week
# without this: (1) the App Store updated Xcode in place on the CI macs and
# bazel half-built against the wrong version with confusing errors; (2)
# during the repair, apple_support's repository rule snapshotted a
# temporarily-present bundle path (/Applications/Xcode-26.6.0-autoupdated.app)
# as DEVELOPER_DIR into cached toolchain configs, which kept failing with
# "xcrun: error: missing DEVELOPER_DIR path" long after that bundle was
# deleted -- repo rules don't re-run when a directory just vanishes.
# --repo_env IS tracked in repo-rule marker files (unlike ambient filesystem
# state), so apple_support's toolchain config now re-runs whenever this value
# changes. With both flags set, host drift or a stale snapshot fails loudly
# at analysis/toolchain-config time instead of half-building.
#
# When the fleet's Xcode is upgraded, bump BOTH flags together.
build:ci-darwin --xcode_version=27.2.0.27B52
common:ci-darwin --repo_env=DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode-27.2.0.app/Contents/Developer

# ==========================================
# SMALLER BINARIES FOR VERIFICATION BUILDS
# ==========================================
# Selected by bazel_verify_only in .buildkite/steps/ci-env.sh, i.e. only by
# commands that build or test for their pass/fail result. Never selected by a
# release build, whose binaries ship and keep their symbols.
#
# Rust executables and test binaries are by far the largest artifacts a CI
# build uploads to the remote cache. They carry no DWARF in a fastbuild (rules_rust
# already compiles with debuginfo=0 there); the weight is the symbol table:
# Rust mangled names are long, and every generic instantiation is an exported
# symbol. Linking with -Cstrip=symbols drops it, which takes 40-50% off each
# macOS binary (see the pull request that added this for a size table).
#
# What is lost: function names in a panic backtrace from a CI run. The panic
# message and its file:line:column are unaffected, since they are compiled
# into the binary as data. To get a symbolised backtrace, re-run the test
# locally, where this config is not selected.
#
# The flag is part of every target-configuration rustc action's cache key,
# including rlib compiles whose output it does not change, so CI's verify-only
# Rust actions are no longer shared with local builds or release builds. They
# still share among CI steps. Exec-configuration tools (proc macros, build
# scripts) are not re-keyed: they use extra_exec_rustc_flag.
build:ci-strip --@rules_rust//rust/settings:extra_rustc_flag=-Cstrip=symbols

# ==========================================
# REMOTE EXECUTION (macOS)
# ==========================================
# Selected by .buildkite/steps/ci-env.sh, after --config=ci-darwin, together
# with --remote_executor and --remote_cache. The endpoint is not written here
# on purpose: it comes from the agent's environment, and the conditions under
# which a step executes remotely are in .buildkite/steps/remote-execution.sh.
# Do not pass --config=ci-remote by hand without an executor: the test strategy
# below names `remote` alone, so every test would fail to schedule.
#
# The disk cache from build:ci-darwin stays on, in front of the remote cache.
# Do not switch it off here. One output base serves both kinds of step, and a
# step that built on the agent leaves outputs that exist only as entries in
# the disk cache. With the disk cache off, a remote step that needs one fails
# with "Lost inputs no longer available remotely" and Bazel starts the build
# again; with it on, the blob is found and uploaded.
# The agent's own core count no longer bounds the build; the workers do.
build:ci-remote --jobs=64
# A remote failure fails the step. ci-env.sh decides once per step whether to
# execute remotely, so a step never mixes worker results with agent results.
# The one exception is the app build in .buildkite/steps/boss-release.sh, which
# overrides this on its command line; the reason is in that script.
#
# That includes an action the scheduler gives up on (no worker connected, a
# worker lost too often, too many retries), which it does within minutes and
# with an error that names the reason; ci-env.sh then says on the build page
# that the executor failed and not the change. An executor that stops
# answering after the step has started is different. Bazel 9.1 puts no
# deadline on an execution call. --grpc_keepalive_time defaults to 60s in
# 9.1; these two lines set 30s / 20s so the values live in this file, and so
# a future Bazel that defaults them to 0 still pings. They are not the bound
# for a stopped hub: measured with keepalive on at the 60s default, a hub
# stopped in the middle of an action was still waiting 17 minutes later.
# The bound is the status watchdog in ci-env.sh, which repeats the
# start-of-step status request while bazel runs and fails the command if the
# hub does not answer, with an annotation that names the executor.
# timeout_in_minutes remains the last resort if the watchdog cannot run.
# Do not add --remote_timeout for it; that flag applies to cache calls only.
# See tools/remote-execution/README.md, "What bounds a stuck action".
build:ci-remote --grpc_keepalive_time=30s
build:ci-remote --grpc_keepalive_timeout=20s
build:ci-remote --noremote_local_fallback
# Tests execute on the workers too; see `test:remote-tests` in .bazelrc.
test:ci-remote --config=remote-tests
# Blobs travel between the agent and the remote cache zstd-compressed. The
# endpoint has to advertise zstd in its capabilities: against one that does
# not, Bazel 9.1 fails the first action that touches the remote cache with
# "--remote_cache_compression requested but remote does not support
# compression". So do not switch compression off on the endpoint while this
# line is here.
build:ci-remote --remote_cache_compression

# ==========================================
# REMOTE EXECUTION (Linux)
# ==========================================
# Selected by .buildkite/steps/ci-env.sh on a Linux x86-64 agent, after
# --config=ci-linux, together with --remote_executor and --remote_cache. As
# with ci-remote above, the endpoint comes from the agent's environment and
# the conditions are in .buildkite/steps/remote-execution.sh.
#
# The disk cache from build:ci-linux stays on, for the reason given under
# ci-remote.
build:ci-remote-linux --jobs=64
# As under ci-remote, including the keepalive lines and what bounds a step
# whose executor stops answering.
build:ci-remote-linux --grpc_keepalive_time=30s
build:ci-remote-linux --grpc_keepalive_timeout=20s
build:ci-remote-linux --noremote_local_fallback
build:ci-remote-linux --remote_cache_compression
# The tag that routes the build: every action asks for a worker whose
# OSFamily is linux, and the scheduler offers an action only to workers that
# declare that value. The macOS and the Linux workers share one scheduler,
# which treats an action with no OSFamily as a macOS action
# (tools/remote-execution/README.md, "Routing"), so this line is what keeps a
# Linux build off the Macs. The property is part of each action's cache key, so
# a step that executes remotely and one that builds on the agent do not share
# results, in either the remote cache or the disk cache.
build:ci-remote-linux --remote_default_exec_properties=OSFamily=linux
# Tests execute on the workers too; see `test:remote-tests` in .bazelrc. This
# replaces `test:linux --strategy=TestRunner=linux-sandbox` for the step, so
# Bazel's linux-sandbox is not around a test any more. The boundary is the
# sandbox every Linux worker runs each action in,
# tools/test-sandbox/linux_remote_action_sandbox.sh, and this line is only
# sound while every worker on the endpoint runs it. Before pointing an agent
# at an endpoint, or adding a worker to one, run the gate in
# tools/test-sandbox/README.md, "Remote execution on Linux workers".
test:ci-remote-linux --config=remote-tests
